From: PhotoCraft AROS port
Subject: [PATCH] Add terminate_global_thread_pool() for exits that join threads

Repository: rayon-rs/rayon, crate rayon-core 1.13.0 (crates.io, checksum
  22e18b0f0062d30d4230b2e85ff77fdfe4326feb054b9783a3460d8435c8ab91).
Date: 2026-10-16
Problem: AROS's pthread library joins every thread, detached ones included,
  in its exit handler (compiler/pthread/pthread.c, __pthread_Exit_Func), so
  that no thread keeps running code that the exit is about to unload. The
  global pool's idle workers never end by themselves, so a program that used
  the global pool never exits. Observed with PhotoCraft on x86_64 ABIv11.
Rationale: expose the release that ThreadPool::drop already performs for
  private pools. The global registry starts with a terminate count of one
  that nothing releases; this releases it once, and the workers exit after
  their current work. Public, target-independent API: a candidate upstream.
Scope: new function; no behaviour change unless it is called.
Upstream-status: candidate (not submitted).
Prerequisites: none.
Verification: probe and PhotoCraft exit on x86_64 ABIv11; see the port's
  evidence reports.
Removal: when rayon-core provides an equivalent.
---
diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
index 3745076..5a31a48 100644
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -137,6 +137,21 @@ pub fn current_num_threads() -> usize {
     crate::registry::Registry::current_num_threads()
 }
 
+/// Lets the global thread pool's worker threads finish and exit once they have
+/// no more work.
+///
+/// Normally the global pool lives until the process ends and the operating
+/// system discards its threads. Some systems instead make the process wait for
+/// every thread before it can end: AROS's pthread library joins all threads,
+/// detached ones included, in its exit handler. There the idle global workers
+/// keep a finished program from ever exiting. Call this once, after the last
+/// use of the global pool, before the process exits. Using the global pool
+/// afterwards is not supported. Calls after the first, and calls when the pool
+/// was never started, do nothing.
+pub fn terminate_global_thread_pool() {
+    crate::registry::terminate_global_registry();
+}
+
 /// Error when initializing a thread pool.
 #[derive(Debug)]
 pub struct ThreadPoolBuildError {
diff --git a/src/registry.rs b/src/registry.rs
index 809976a..faaa66e 100644
--- a/src/registry.rs
+++ b/src/registry.rs
@@ -206,6 +206,24 @@ where
     result
 }
 
+/// Ends the global pool's worker threads once they run out of work (see
+/// [`crate::terminate_global_thread_pool`]). Only the first call has an effect.
+pub(super) fn terminate_global_registry() {
+    static TERMINATED: std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool = std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool::new(false);
+    if !THE_REGISTRY_SET.is_completed() || TERMINATED.swap(true, Ordering::AcqRel) {
+        return;
+    }
+    // SAFETY: as in `global_registry`: a shared reference, taken only after the
+    // `call_once` that wrote the registry, with no mutable access afterwards.
+    let the_registry = unsafe { &*ptr::addr_of!(THE_REGISTRY) };
+    if let Some(registry) = the_registry.as_ref() {
+        // The global registry starts with a terminate count of one that nothing
+        // ever releases; this is that release, as `ThreadPool::drop` does for
+        // its own registry.
+        registry.terminate();
+    }
+}
+
 fn default_global_registry() -> Result<Arc<Registry>, ThreadPoolBuildError> {
     let result = Registry::new(ThreadPoolBuilder::new());
 
